      <!-- Section 2 -->
      <section id="regulatory" class="section">
        <h2>2. Environmental and Regulatory Context</h2>
        <p>The transition from hydrofluorocarbons to hydrofluoroolefins is driven by strict environmental regulations at both national and international levels. Under the Kigali Amendment to the Montreal Protocol, which is managed by the <a href="https://ozone.unep.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UNEP Ozone Secretariat</a>, signatory nations are required to phase down HFC consumption to reduce greenhouse gas emissions. Because HFC-134a has a high global warming potential (GWP₁₀₀ = 1430), it is targeted for phase-out in non-essential aerosol products, making low-GWP alternatives necessary.</p>
        <p>In the United States, the EPA Significant New Alternatives Policy (SNAP) program, details of which are available via the <a href="https://www.epa.gov/snap" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">US EPA SNAP Program</a>, lists HFO-1234ze as an acceptable substitute for HFC-134a in consumer aerosols. Similarly, the European Union's F-Gas Regulation has established strict allocation limits that have increased the cost of high-GWP gases, encouraging manufacturers to adopt sustainable options like HFOs. These environmental parameters are discussed further in our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/propellant-influence-on-aerosol-performance" target="_top">propellant influence on aerosol performance</a>.</p>
        
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Kigali Amendment Goals:</strong> Aiming to reduce HFC consumption by over 80% by 2047 to prevent up to 0.5°C of global warming by 2100.</li>
          <li><strong>EPA SNAP Status:</strong> Lists HFO-1234ze as acceptable for use in technical dusters, spray paints, and industrial formulations.</li>
          <li><strong>F-Gas Compliance:</strong> Restricts the use of fluorinated greenhouse gases with GWPs above 150 in most retail consumer aerosols, accelerating HFO adoption in Europe.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <section id="flammability" class="section">
        <h2>3. Flammability and Safety Dynamics</h2>
        <p>A key advantage of HFO-1234ze over hydrocarbon propellants (such as propane and butane) is its non-flammable classification under standard temperature and pressure conditions. According to standard testing protocols like <a href="https://www.astm.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM International</a> E681, HFO-1234ze does not exhibit flammability limits in air at 20°C and 101.3 kPa. This characteristic makes it suitable for applications that require non-flammable propellants for safety or regulatory compliance. For details on flammability metrics, see our overview of <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosols" target="_top">aerosols</a>.</p>
        <p>However, at elevated temperatures (typically above 30°C), HFO-1234ze can form weakly flammable mixtures with air when exposed to a high-energy ignition source. The lower flammability limit (LFL) is approximately 7.0% by volume under these conditions, and its low burning velocity (less than 1.2 cm/s) classifies it as a Class 2L (weakly flammable) gas under ISO 10156 standards. Consequently, while HFO-1234ze is significantly safer than LPG or dimethyl ether, product developers must still evaluate flammability behavior when combining it with flammable solvents like ethanol or acetone.</p>

        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--amber); background: #fffbeb; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
          <strong style="color: var(--amber); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Rule of Thumb</strong>
          When formulating with HFO-1234ze, avoid using high concentrations of highly volatile flammable solvents (like acetone or methylal) if a completely non-flammable finished aerosol is required. The solvent vapors can form flammable mixtures that override the vapor-suppressing characteristics of the propellant.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Infographic Image -->
      <div class="image-slot">
