      <!-- Section 4 -->
      <section id="solvency" class="section">
        <h2>4. Solvency Characteristics & Compatibility</h2>
        <p>HFO-1234ze has low solubility in water and a low Kauri-Butanol (KB) value, which limits its ability to dissolve active concentrates directly. Unlike dimethyl ether (DME), which acts as a powerful polar solvent, HFO-1234ze behaves as a pressurized diluent in most aerosol formulations. This requires the use of co-solvents to dissolve active ingredients and maintain a homogeneous single-phase system inside the container. To review phase separation mechanics, see our technical article on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-chemistry-101" target="_top">aerosol chemistry 101</a>.</p>
        <p>In paint and coating formulations, the low solvency of HFO-1234ze can lead to resin precipitation if the solvent package is not carefully balanced. Formulators typically include active solvents such as esters (butyl acetate) or ketones (methyl ethyl ketone) to maintain resin solubility. In water-based formulations, surfactant systems must be optimized to stabilize the emulsion, as HFO-1234ze can separate into a distinct liquid layer if the emulsion is disrupted. This separation can cause inconsistent discharge rates and spray performance.</p>

        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--primary); background: #f0f9ff; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
          <strong style="color: var(--primary); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Key Insight</strong>
          Because HFO-1234ze is denser than water (liquid density ~1.17 g/cm³), any separated propellant phase will settle to the bottom of the can. This is the opposite of hydrocarbon propellants, which float on water. Formulations must be engineered to prevent this phase separation and ensure consistent spray delivery.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5 -->
      <section id="compatibility" class="section">
        <h2>5. Valve and Elastomer Gasket Guidelines</h2>
        <p>Elastomer compatibility is an important factor when selecting valve components for HFO-1234ze formulations. The propellant exhibits good compatibility with common valve gasket materials, causing minimal swelling or physical degradation during long-term storage tests. This helps prevent leakages and maintains consistent valve seal performance. Further details on elastomer performance are available on the <a href="https://webbook.nist.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIST WebBook</a> thermodynamic database.</p>
        <p>Comparative studies show that EPDM (ethylene propylene diene monomer) and butyl rubber gaskets show very low volume change when exposed to pure HFO-1234ze, making them suitable choices for technical sprays and dusters. However, viton and certain nitrile rubbers can experience minor swelling if the formulation contains high concentrations of polar co-solvents. Valve selection should always include storage trials of the complete formulation to verify seal integrity over the shelf life of the product.</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>EPDM Gaskets:</strong> Highly recommended for pure HFO-1234ze systems; exhibits low swelling and maintains physical properties over time.</li>
          <li><strong>Butyl Rubber:</strong> Excellent barrier properties with low gas permeability, helping minimize propellant loss through the valve seals during storage.</li>
          <li><strong>Nitrile (Buna-N):</strong> Acceptable for many formulations, but compatibility should be validated if active co-solvents are present.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6 -->
      <section id="manufacturing" class="section">
        <h2>6. Scale-Up & Plant Requirements</h2>
        <p>Integrating HFO-1234ze into existing aerosol filling lines requires evaluating plant safety and ventilation systems. Although HFO-1234ze is classified as non-flammable under standard ambient conditions, its potential to exhibit weak flammability at elevated temperatures means that safety precautions are still necessary. Manufacturing plants should ensure adequate air exchange rates in the gassing area to prevent the accumulation of heavy propellant vapors. For a detailed guide on filling plant setup, see <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-manufacturing-101-guide" target="_top">aerosol manufacturing 101</a>.</p>
        <p>Because HFO-1234ze has a higher boiling point (-19.0°C) than HFC-134a (-26.1°C), the liquid propellant requires less sub-cooling during the charging stage to prevent flashing in the metering cylinders. Gassing pressures must be monitored to ensure complete filling without overpressurizing the containers. The finished aerosol cans must pass standard quality checks, including hot water bath testing to verify crimp integrity and detect any leakage prior to packaging.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ SECTION -->
      <section class="box" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 30px; margin: 40px 0;" id="faq">
        <h2 style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 24px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; color: var(--text-primary);">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px;">
          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What is HFO-1234ze and why is it used as an aerosol propellant?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">HFO-1234ze (trans-1,3,3,3-tetrafluoropropene) is a fourth-generation hydrofluoroolefin propellant designed to replace high-GWP hydrofluorocarbons (HFCs) like HFC-134a. It is widely adopted due to its near-zero Global Warming Potential (GWP &lt; 1), non-flammable status under standard conditions, and compatibility with standard aerosol valve systems.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Is HFO-1234ze flammable under typical operating conditions?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Under standard atmospheric conditions at 20°C and 101.3 kPa, HFO-1234ze is classified as non-flammable by standard ASTM E681 tests. However, it exhibits a lower flammability limit (LFL) at elevated temperatures (above 30°C), classifying it as weakly flammable (Category 2L or Class 3 flammability depending on regional standards) under ISO 10156 rules.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">How do the vapor pressure profiles of HFO-1234ze and HFC-134a compare?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">HFO-1234ze exhibits a lower vapor pressure profile than HFC-134a across the entire operating temperature range. At 20°C, HFO-1234ze has a vapor pressure of approximately 4.2 bar (absolute), compared to 5.7 bar for HFC-134a, which requires formulation adjustments to maintain consistent atomization and discharge rates.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What are the environmental regulations driving the transition to HFO-1234ze?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">The transition is primarily driven by international environmental agreements like the Kigali Amendment to the Montreal Protocol, which mandates a phase-down of high-GWP HFCs. Regional regulations, such as the EU F-Gas Regulation and US EPA SNAP rules, impose strict restrictions on using HFC-134a in consumer aerosol applications.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Are standard aerosol valve gaskets compatible with HFO-1234ze?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Yes, HFO-1234ze exhibits excellent compatibility with common aerosol valve gasket elastomers. Tests show it causes minimal swelling in EPDM and butyl rubber gaskets compared to other propellants, though minor swelling can occur when paired with aggressive co-solvents like dimethyl ether or ethanol.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="padding-bottom: 8px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Does HFO-1234ze require specialized gassing equipment on aerosol filling lines?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">While HFO-1234ze does not require the strict explosion-proof equipment mandatory for highly flammable LPG propellants, manufacturing plants must still adapt ventilation and leak-detection systems. This is necessary because HFO-1234ze is heavier than air and can accumulate in low areas, and it can exhibit weak flammability at elevated temperatures.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

